in some ways I've always been interested  in people who have a hard time  communicating their emotions and maybe  the the sort of quote/unquote normal way  you know something with the musical is  you you kind of can resort to song and  dance to communicate those emotions in a  way that words can't and with Neal I  think it was there was such poetry I  think for me just looking at his life in  the sort of story of someone who in many  cases seemed to sublimate his emotions  into his work and into this passion that  he had ever since he was a little boy of  Av passion for aviation and this is  someone who learned how to fly before he  learned how to drive Ryan and I spent  some time up in the cornfields in Ohio  where he grew up at his old farmhouse  where he grew up and you know it's not  in the movie but it kind of informed a  lot of what we did in the movie where  you just go there you see these see the  sea of you know flat cornfields and this  giant sky you can kind of immediately  understand why Ohio produces more  astronauts than any other any other  state in the u.s. any other part of the  world we went in to the astronaut Hall  of Fame we were all there to do some  some training and the the young ladies  showing us around was like hey you know  all your guys are in there you can go  see the plaque of your guy and and we  went in and I might get into some  spoilers here but it is historical so we  got in there and I play Elliott C so I  was looking around for Elliott and then  I realized like all the plaques have  mission patches underneath them and  Elliott never got to fly a mission and  it it struck me pretty profoundly being  in that room and I missed Elliott when I  was in there looking at all the  astronauts but all the all the  characters in this are so rich and we're  lucky enough at least myself personally  for deke slayton there's so much  information and there's there's so much  you can go to youtube and look at all  these conferences and and his his his  past is really well recorded and so you  could really get in there and find  things to ground yourself so I really  enjoyed doing this character because I  felt like I was I had cinderblocks on my  feet to get to play a woman that is both  strong and on the team and and not in  the shadows and then also shown to be  vulnerable I mean that's like an  incredible character so yeah that was  definitely what attracted to me the role  and I think Josh really made a point of  that in his story to have it be about  the family not just the man yeah Janet  and Pat and all the women who were left  at home were extraordinary but I think  it takes a filmmaker like Damian who  those stories are there it's not like  they didn't they those women were always  there just nobody was interested you  know the story was here the story was  with the men who were going to the moon  and that's what I think the films are  amazing doing is the humanity of the men  who were going was also not that  important to anybody nobody really  wanted to know that they had lives and  that they had arguments with their wives  and their kids and you know all those  things and I think it takes a filmmaker  to shine the light on that and say that  that's the real story really that's a  story we wanted to know we don't want to  know what we've been told and fed and  you know makes us believe in heroes and  that all these people have superpowers  we want you know and I have I don't I  can't read a script that's like that I'm  afraid if you look at it in terms of the  rocks that we collected the experiments  that we set up and those type of things  it's a it's a it's a tough question but  if you look at it in terms of what would  what I think we see which is it inspired  and motivated an entire generation of  people to want to be better it made them  want to work harder because great things  could happen and and dreams could come  true and if you just take that and you  multiply that by the number of people  across the world it's a very very  powerful thing it was I mean it was it  was sort of an extension of that space  camp in the sense that we were given  every opportunity to  to feel what what they felt as close as  as close as we could get while we were  filming a movie you know when to have al  worden who was Apollo 15 astronaut right  they're telling us you know this is when  you flip the switch or and then to look  out the window and you see pretty much  what we're seeing in the movie was  incredible the theremin was actually a  really big find personally because I was  looking for points of connection with  Neil and I wasn't it was hard to relate  to you know his genius his courage his  humility because those are so  extraordinary I was looking for things  that that I we had in common and and we  have the theremin nobody really talks  about or at least I haven't seen it yet  is is how supportive the wives were of  one another that was that was a that was  a key piece of making that work and  there there's a scene where I think you  and then you two in Maryland see or  think to think in your house and that  kind of stuff happened all the time and  the the wives really were the one  because the guys were gone and we're  gone locked and and they were the only  ones that could you know sort of relate  to one another and help each other out  and that's that's a very important story  and a very important contribution that  making the whole thing work

